1b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 937µs | 0.02% ] round: 7\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 664µs | 0.01% ] round: 8\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 599µs | 0.01% ] round: 9\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 442µs | 0.01% ] round: 10\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 220µs | 0.00% ] round: 11\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 202µs | 0.00% ] round: 12\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 186µs | 0.00% ] round: 13\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 165µs | 0.00% ] round: 14\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 160µs | 0.00% ] round: 15\n\u001b[1;32mINFO    \u001b[0m │        │  │     ┝━ LogupZerocheck::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval [ 154µs | 0.00% ] round: 16","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/georg/stark-backend","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01D6dhML6vZPbinMSfUmzTxB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"With `buffer_size > 0` filter, 0 candidates are selected (all candidates have buffer_size > 0). So the batching is disabled entirely and it completes fine.\n\nThis means ALL batchable small AIRs have `buffer_size > 0`. So the batch kernel will always use intermediates.\n\nLet me reconsider the approach. The key problem is that the batched kernel needs to allocate intermediates for all AIRs simultaneously, which far exceeds L2 cache.\n\nBut wait - the existing per-AIR code also uses GLOBAL intermediates (the same `d_intermediates` pointer pattern). \n\nThe per-AIR path processes one AIR at a time per thread, so only one AIR's intermediates are active at once. With 8 concurrent streams, that's at most 8 AIRs' worth of intermediates in flight. The batched kernel, though, loads all AIRs' intermediates into the arena at once and processes blocks from different AIRs concurrently, so the entire working set is the sum of all intermediates. This is the fundamental constraint—the sequential per-AIR approach gets L2 reuse because each AIR's data fits, but batching can't avoid having everything in memory simultaneously.\n\nThis mirrors the exact problem we hit with `batch-global-gkr-input-eval`: the aggregate intermediates buffer for ~211 AIRs was 576 MB, which completely overwhelmed the 72 MB L2 cache and tanked performance from 528ms to 694-944ms. The lesson there was that kernel launch overhead is negligible—the real win from the per-AIR sequential approach is the intermediates buffer reuse that batching fundamentally can't replicate. Round 0 batching has the same issue: even a small group of AIRs' intermediates exceeds L2 capacity, destroying the cache efficiency that makes the current approach fast.\n\nThe OOM blocker got resolved, but that doesn't address the L2 cache thrashing that makes batching slower anyway. Though wait—the original hang was 12+ minutes, which is way beyond what cache thrashing alone would cause. I'm wondering if there's a CUDA deadlock happening: the batch eval runs on the default stream, then `current_stream_sync()` waits for it to finish. If the batch kernel is somehow blocked waiting for a resource held by another stream, that would deadlock. But the batch eval runs before spawning worker threads, so no other streams should be active yet. Let me think about what else could cause the hang...\n\nMaybe `build_logup_rules_for_air` is just extremely slow for certain AIR configurations. I should add tracing to pinpoint exactly where the hang occurs. Actually, let me try a simpler diagnostic: remove the logup batch entirely and only run zerocheck batch eval to see if that's where the bottleneck is.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1318,"cache_read_input_tokens":152672,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1318},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa62649b-653a-4cec-aa74-3431ae869005","uuid":"4e8295c0-8a11-4dab-804e-cd84db412d07"}
