{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SNnjUnqqGSXFfc4ATWsSPh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The batch eval completed, but no THREAD messages have appeared. This means the Phase 2 worker threads are hanging. None of them have completed processing even a single AIR.\n\nThe hang is in the Phase 2 worker threads, which process both batched (extraction only) and non-batched (full processing) AIRs. Since no thread has printed any output, the hang happens on the very first AIR processed by each thread.\n\nWith round-robin distribution on 8 threads, thread 0 gets items 0, 8, 16, ... Thread 0's first item (item 0) is the largest AIR (sorted by descending height). \n\nSo the hang is actually in `process_air_round0()` for a non-batched AIR—not the batch extraction itself. The batch eval completes successfully but somehow corrupts GPU state, causing the subsequent per-AIR processing to hang. This looks like the same \"Rust optimizer interference\" pattern I've seen before where the batch code works fine but breaks downstream operations.\n\nThe problem seems to be that batch eval allocates temporary buffers and frees them via `cudaFreeAsync` on the default stream, but Phase 2 workers use per-thread streams. Even though `current_stream_sync()` waits for the default stream, those async frees might not be visible to the per-thread streams, leaving the memory pool in an inconsistent state.\n\nActually, wait—`BatchEvalState` still owns the output buffers (`zc_groups` and `logup_groups`) when it's returned, so those should remain valid for the workers to read. The intermediate scratch arenas are also kept alive, and since the eval kernels completed, they're safe to access. The intermediates are only needed during kernel execution, so after the reduce step completes, the output buffers have the final results.\n\nBut keeping those intermediates around might be creating memory pressure. With 171 AIRs and roughly 300 MB of intermediates each, that's a lot of GPU memory still allocated during Phase 2. When the per-AIR processing starts allocating its own intermediate buffers across 8 worker threads, each potentially requesting the max buffer size, we could be hitting memory limits. Let me try freeing the intermediates right after the sync completes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":278,"cache_read_input_tokens":173079,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":278},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa62649b-653a-4cec-aa74-3431ae869005","uuid":"3fea4142-b41e-4096-95d2-413ba1311063"}
