{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012hvuC7DbA8WcoKNEuGNQAg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still hanging! Even when I DON'T use batch extraction and just use the normal `process_air_round0` path for all AIRs, the process hangs. The issue is that the mere PRESENCE of the batch eval (which runs on the default stream) corrupts GPU state for subsequent per-AIR processing on the per-thread streams.\n\nThis is the same pattern described in the log for `batch-round0-descriptor-arrays`:\n> adding the Rust orchestration code that routes small AIRs to the batched path consistently caused CUDA illegal memory access errors at APC 300, even when the batched code path was never executed\n\n\nAnd the `batch-round0-caller-routing` attempt also hit pre-existing GPU OOM in gkr_input. The fundamental issue is that running the batch eval kernel changes CUDA pool state, and subsequent per-AIR processing on different streams fails—even though the batch eval completes and syncs successfully, the VPMM pool ends up in a state that causes hangs on later allocations and kernels. I'm going to try dropping the entire batch state immediately after the sync, then let Phase 2 process all AIRs normally without extraction, to isolate whether just running the batch eval itself causes the hang.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":110,"cache_read_input_tokens":178451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":110},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa62649b-653a-4cec-aa74-3431ae869005","uuid":"d6516266-ef13-4266-b735-4d6da7875063"}
